  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two ways of storing carbon dioxide in basaltic rock by turning it into solid carbonate minerals underground. In both cases, CO₂ is injected through a well into subsurface basalt, where it reacts with the rock and eventually mineralizes.\n\nOn the left, CO₂ is first mixed with water at the surface, producing dissolved CO₂ that is sent down into the basalt. The arrows suggest the fluid spreads through the rock and forms dispersed carbonate minerals within the formation. This route emphasizes injection as a dissolved phase, likely to promote rapid reaction with the basalt.\n\nOn the right, CO₂ is injected directly as a dense liquid or supercritical fluid beneath a cap rock. The pink plume shows the CO₂ moving laterally and downward into a flooded basalt layer, while small white particles indicate carbonate minerals forming where the CO₂ contacts water in the rock. The cap rock appears to act as a seal that helps confine the injected CO₂.\n\nOverall, the image highlights the contrast between injecting dissolved CO₂ versus injecting concentrated CO₂, with both approaches aiming for permanent mineral trapping in basalt.",
